GNU Coreutils FreeBASIC port
File: fb_port/patches/README

freebasic-shared-data-alias.patch preserves the compiler change required for
C2FB's initialized and tentative global definitions. It accepts Alias on
non-private shared data and exports that storage with the requested C symbol.
It also includes the Linux C/FreeBASIC linked regression under tests/functions/.
It intentionally excludes other changes present in the development checkout.

The patch was extracted from the jasonkfirth/fbc source checkout based on:

  d2b8a2ee6fb1a4a3fadef47e9620473b944f46e6
  https://github.com/jasonkfirth/fbc.git

From a clean FreeBASIC checkout, use the absolute path to this patch:

  git apply --check /path/to/Freebasic-gnu_coreutils/fb_port/patches/freebasic-shared-data-alias.patch
  git apply /path/to/Freebasic-gnu_coreutils/fb_port/patches/freebasic-shared-data-alias.patch

Set COREUTILS_FBC_SOURCE to the patched checkout and run port.py compiler as
described in ../README. The existing development checkout already contains
this change; do not apply it twice. An installed FreeBASIC compiler and its
runtime are still needed to build the patched compiler.

The .bmk regression runs through the FreeBASIC multi-module test harness. Its
Linux case links shared-alias-data.c with shared-alias-data.bas and checks
initialization and writes in both directions. Other platforms use the noop
entry because this regression currently verifies the Linux linker behavior.

end of fb_port/patches/README
